<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Xinjiang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Xinjiang is a large region in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungaria">Dzungaria</a>, historically home to oasis cities on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a>. Its eastern parts formed part of the domains of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Empire</a> and its successors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> conquered the region in the mid-eighteenth century, and Xinjiang's incorporation helped define the territorial boundaries of modern China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xinjiang.jpg" alt="Xinjiang" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HaziiDozen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B9%8C%E9%B2%81%E6%9C%A8%E9%BD%90%E4%BA%BA%E6%B0%91%E5%85%AC%E5%9B%AD%E5%B0%81%E9%9D%A2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
